// HumanityOS environment Layer 1: each world's analytic climate
//
// Layer 1 is the continuous base field under all weather: the air temperature,
// the air pressure and the prevailing wind at any point on a world, as a pure
// function of WHERE (direction from the body centre, altitude, land or sea)
// and WHEN (the fraction of the game year). It has no storage and no state.
// The weather system's condition (a storm, a clear spell) is the deviation on
// top of it, and the environment regions (Layer 2) place that deviation.
//
// ONE ROW PER WORLD, which is the point: a new world is a row, not code
// (docs/design/infinite-of-x.md). A world without a row falls back to the
// generic per-body model in src/systems/body_environment.rs (catalog mean
// temperature, a latitude cosine, the lapse) with Earth's pressure column
// where it has air; that is the honest "we have no climate data for it" arm.
//
// Design: docs/design/environment-fields.md, "Layer 1 as built".
// CPU model (f64): src/systems/env_layer1.rs
// GPU twin (f32):  assets/shaders/pbr/00-bindings-vertex.wgsl, EnvClimate and
//                  the env_l1_* functions, held in lockstep by a test that
//                  RUNS the shader functions (env_layer1.rs tests).
//
// FIELDS
//
// Temperature at sea level, C, with x = sin(latitude) and tau = years since
// the northern winter solstice:
//     T(x, tau) = sea_level_mean_c + p2_c * (3x^2 - 1)/2
//               + x * (A cos(2 pi tau) + B sin(2 pi tau))
// where (A, B) is season_<hemisphere>_<surface>, blended by the land fraction
// at the place. The form is North, Cahalan and Coakley 1981 ("Energy balance
// climate models", Rev. Geophys. Space Phys. 19:91-121, equation 51 and
// Table 1). It is exactly zero seasonally at the equator, so the per-hemisphere
// pairs meet continuously there.
//   north_winter_solstice_year_fraction
//                 where the northern winter solstice falls in the GAME year.
//                 The game year is 120 days of four 30-day seasons starting
//                 with Spring at day 0 (src/systems/time.rs). Read as
//                 astronomical seasons, Spring begins at the March equinox, so
//                 the northern winter solstice is the first day of Winter:
//                 day 90 of 120 = 0.75.
//
// The air column above sea level (the 1976 US Standard Atmosphere's two lowest
// layers, generalised so another world is a row):
//   surface_pressure_kpa   pressure at sea level (or the world's datum)
//   gravity_ms2            standard gravity at the datum
//   molar_mass_kg_per_mol  mean molar mass of the air
//   geopotential_radius_km altitude is converted to geopotential height,
//                          H = h / (1 + h / r0), as the standard does
//   lapse_k_per_km         temperature fall per km below lapse_break_km
//   lapse_break_km         top of the lower layer (Earth's tropopause)
//   upper_lapse_k_per_km   fall per km above it (0 = isothermal)
// The column starts from the Layer 1 SEA-LEVEL temperature of the place, so a
// cold polar column loses pressure faster with height than a tropical one, as
// the real atmosphere does (the hypsometric equation: a layer's thickness is
// proportional to its mean temperature). With a 15 C sea level it IS the
// standard atmosphere, which a test pins.
//
// Prevailing wind, m/s, as a 16-term sine series in colatitude theta:
//     u(theta, tau) = sum_{n=1..16} sin(n theta) (mean_n + cos_n cos 2 pi tau
//                                                  + sin_n sin 2 pi tau)
// u = toward the east, v = toward the north (the reanalysis convention). Every
// term vanishes at both poles, where a wind has no single east component. A
// series rather than a lookup table because the GPU twin then needs no array
// indexed at run time (which a shader would copy into every invocation's
// private memory: the frame-cost lesson in docs/design/frame-cost-arc.md).
// Fewer than 16 numbers means the rest are 0; an empty list means no known
// prevailing wind (the weather's own wind is all there is).

(
    worlds: [
        // ── Earth ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
        // Every number below except the column constants comes from ONE public
        // dataset through a checked-in script, so it can be reproduced:
        //   node scripts/climate-fit.js
        // NCEP/NCAR Reanalysis 1 (Kalnay et al. 1996, Bull. Amer. Meteor. Soc.
        // 77:437-471), long-term monthly means 1991-2020, read 2026-09-27 from
        // NOAA PSL's OPeNDAP server. "NCEP-NCAR Reanalysis 1 data provided by
        // the NOAA PSL, Boulder, Colorado, USA, from their website at
        // https://psl.noaa.gov". US government data, public domain.
        (
            body: "earth",
            // Annual mean over SEA cells (the ocean surface is sea level; land
            // cells carry terrain height). North et al. 1981 Table 1 has 14.9
            // and -28.0 from 1970s data; these agree to 0.7 C and 1.6 C.
            // Equator 28.8 C, 45 degrees 9.0 C, pole -10.8 C.
            sea_level_mean_c: 15.59,
            p2_c: -26.43,
            north_winter_solstice_year_fraction: 0.75,
            // Seasonal (cos, sin) coefficients per unit sin(latitude), fitted per
            // hemisphere and surface. North et al.'s symmetric fit, (-13.2, -8.1),
            // sits between the northern land and sea pairs, as it should for a
            // hemisphere that is about 40 percent land. The southern pairs are
            // far smaller over sea: the Southern Ocean barely has seasons.
            // Warmest moment in the north: land 0.57 of a year after the solstice
            // (mid July), sea 0.63 (early August), the ocean lagging as it does.
            season_north_sea: (-6.47, -6.79),
            season_north_land: (-18.12, -8.59),
            season_south_sea: (-3.38, -3.82),
            season_south_land: (-14.22, -2.57),
            // The 1976 US Standard Atmosphere (NOAA/NASA/USAF, NOAA-S/T 76-1562):
            // sea-level pressure 101.325 kPa, g0 9.80665, M0 28.9644 g/mol,
            // r0 6356.766 km, lapse 6.5 K per geopotential km to 11 km, then
            // isothermal to 20 km. Above 20 km this column stays isothermal, a
            // simplification: nothing that breathes lives there.
            surface_pressure_kpa: 101.325,
            gravity_ms2: 9.80665,
            molar_mass_kg_per_mol: 0.0289644,
            geopotential_radius_km: 6356.766,
            lapse_k_per_km: 6.5,
            lapse_break_km: 11.0,
            upper_lapse_k_per_km: 0.0,
            // Zonal-mean 10 m wind, annual mean and first harmonic, area-weighted
            // rms error of the series 0.35 m/s (u) and 0.18 m/s (v). What it
            // holds, annual mean: trades from the east-north-east at 16 N (u -3.2,
            // v -1.3) and east-south-east at 16 S (u -4.0, v +1.9), meeting near
            // 5 N; westerlies peaking at 47 N (u +1.7, weak: the northern land
            // masses drag them) and 52 S (u +7.1, the Roaring Forties); easterlies
            // off Antarctica near 71 S (u -1.9). The Arctic zonal mean is nearly
            // calm in this data: the textbook "polar easterlies" of the north are
            // regional, not zonal, at 10 m.
            wind_u_mean: [-0.358, -0.921, 2.517, -0.496, -1.083, 1.140, -1.857, 0.674, 0.565, -0.055, 0.112, -0.426, 0.480, -0.138, 0.161, -0.179],
            wind_u_cos: [-0.062, 0.158, 0.244, 0.110, 0.079, -0.607, -0.196, 0.614, 0.036, -0.384, 0.069, 0.061, -0.056, 0.039, -0.124, 0.026],
            wind_u_sin: [-0.111, 0.099, 0.011, -0.036, -0.007, -0.466, 0.024, 0.654, -0.017, -0.450, -0.018, 0.013, 0.013, -0.087, -0.117, 0.100],
            wind_v_mean: [0.142, -0.101, -0.294, 0.509, 0.445, -1.086, 0.343, -0.113, 0.222, 0.024, -0.093, 0.184, -0.054, 0.188, -0.075, 0.018],
            wind_v_cos: [-0.479, 0.051, 0.300, 0.288, -0.416, 0.015, 0.014, 0.020, 0.055, 0.044, -0.133, -0.017, 0.140, -0.100, -0.006, -0.060],
            wind_v_sin: [-0.377, -0.089, 0.372, 0.043, -0.361, -0.058, 0.209, 0.063, -0.009, -0.027, -0.131, 0.084, -0.015, -0.047, -0.045, 0.028],
        ),
        // ── Mars ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
        // What is sourced is the COLUMN. NASA Mars Fact Sheet (NSSDCA, page last
        // updated 2025-05-19, read 2026-09-27): surface pressure "6.36 mb at mean
        // radius (variable from 4.0 to 8.7 mb depending on season)", average
        // temperature "~214 K (-59 C)", mean molecular weight 43.49, surface
        // gravity 3.73 m/s2, volumetric mean radius 3389.5 km, scale height 11.0
        // km. Lapse rates: NASA Glenn Research Center, "Mars Atmosphere Model -
        // Metric Units": T = -31 - 0.000998 h below 7,000 m and
        // T = -23.4 - 0.00222 h above it. Check: R T / (g M) at 214 K is 10.97
        // km, the fact sheet's 11.0 km scale height.
        //
        // NOT sourced, so ZERO rather than invented: how Mars's temperature
        // varies with latitude and season, and its prevailing winds by latitude
        // (the fact sheet's "2-7 m/s (summer), 5-10 m/s (fall), 17-30 m/s (dust
        // storm)" are Viking lander readings at two sites, not a zonal profile).
        // A Mars climatology fit is the follow-up; until then Mars is flat in
        // latitude, its day/night swing still comes from the body model, and
        // its wind is the weather's own.
        (
            body: "mars",
            sea_level_mean_c: -59.15,
            p2_c: 0.0,
            north_winter_solstice_year_fraction: 0.75,
            season_north_sea: (0.0, 0.0),
            season_north_land: (0.0, 0.0),
            season_south_sea: (0.0, 0.0),
            season_south_land: (0.0, 0.0),
            surface_pressure_kpa: 0.636,
            gravity_ms2: 3.73,
            molar_mass_kg_per_mol: 0.04349,
            geopotential_radius_km: 3389.5,
            lapse_k_per_km: 0.998,
            lapse_break_km: 7.0,
            upper_lapse_k_per_km: 2.22,
            wind_u_mean: [],
            wind_u_cos: [],
            wind_u_sin: [],
            wind_v_mean: [],
            wind_v_cos: [],
            wind_v_sin: [],
        ),
        // The Moon has no row on purpose: it has no air, so no pressure and no
        // wind, and its surface temperature is set by sunlight on bare regolith,
        // swinging by hundreds of kelvin between lunar day and night: a function
        // of LOCAL solar time that needs per-longitude sun geometry. It stays on
        // the airless body model until that exists.
    ],
)
